    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d207">

                <pb facs="0241" n="237" xml:id="pg237"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/15340: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Dooman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-09-06T19:00:00+09:00">September 6,
                            1939—7 [<hi rendition="#italic">5</hi>] p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 6—11:08 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>459. Our 458, September 5, 7 p.m.<anchor n="6" xml:id="d207-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>1. There is quoted in the telegram immediately following<anchor n="7"
                        xml:id="d207-f2"/> the text of a telegram which the British Ambassador
                    despatched today to London suggesting in outline the reply which should be sent
                    to the Japanese Government’s note quoted in our telegram under reference. The
                    French Ambassador is telegraphing in substantially similar terms to his
                    Government.</p>
                <p>[For paraphrase of remainder of telegram No. 459, see <ref
                        target="frus:frus1931-41v02/pg_10">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, Japan, 1931–1941, volume II,
                        page 10</ref>.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dooman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d207-f1" n="6" xml:id="d207-f1-content">
                <p>
                    <ref target="frus1931-41v02#pg_9">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, Japan, 1931–1941, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, p. 9</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d207-f2" n="7" xml:id="d207-f2-content">
                <p>No. 460, September 6, 7 p.m. (not printed); in this telegram, the Chargé in Japan
                    quoted the British Ambassador’s telegram suggesting that his Government inform
                    the Japanese Government of its intention to retain its troops and ships in the
                    Far East as long as needed to protect British interests and that it was anxious
                    to continue efforts to settle differences with Japan by friendly negotiations
                    (793.94/15348).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
